    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that makes sense. One thing though—we've been seeing these random, seemingly \"random\" spikes in lead time where a shipment just disappears from the tracking or takes twice as long for no apparent reason, even when the supplier says everything is fine. How should I factor in those specific, weirdly unpredictable delays into the safety stock calculation? Is there a specific way to model that kind of inconsistency?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those sudden, seemingly random delays are classic signs of a poorly managed supply chain, and they are much trickier to model than just standard deviation. You'll need to move beyond simple historical averages and consider a more robust approach, perhaps by using a service-level-driven approach that incorporates a higher-than-usual variability factor. If you can't quantify the *why* of the delay, you must quantify the *impact* by increasing your safety stock buffer significantly.\n\nFor those truly erratic spikes, you might want to look at modeling the lead time as a non-normal distribution, or, more practically, use a scenario planning approach. Calculate your ROP based on a \"worst-case plausible scenario\" lead time (e.g., the 95th or 99th percentile of observed delays) rather than just the average. This is a practical way to account for the \"disappearing shipment\" events without needing a perfect statistical model for every single unpredictable glitch.",
      "source": "target",
